WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.220
Part 3. You will hear two veterinary science students called Diana and Tim

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.500
discussing their work placements and their course modules. First you have some

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.580
time to look at questions 21 to 26.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.839
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26.

00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:15.720
So Tim, we have to do a short summary of our work experience on a farm. Right, my

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.760
farm was great but arranging the work experience was hard. One problem was it

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.839
was miles away and I don't drive and also I'd really wanted a placement for a

00:01:26.839 --> 00:01:33.199
month but I could only get one for two weeks. I was lucky the farmer let me stay

00:01:33.199 --> 00:01:38.760
on the farm so I didn't have to travel but finding the right sort of farm to

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:44.760
apply to wasn't easy. No, they don't seem to have websites do they? I found mine

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.519
through a friend of my mother's but it wasn't easy. No. My farm was mostly

00:01:50.519 --> 00:01:56.120
livestock, especially sheep. I really enjoyed helping out with them. I was up

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:00.839
most of one night helping a sheep deliver a lamb. On your own? No, the farmer

00:02:00.839 --> 00:02:06.680
was there and he told me what to do. It wasn't a straightforward birth but I

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.800
managed. It was a great feeling to see the lamb stagger to its feet and start

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:17.759
feeding almost straight away and to know that it was okay. Then another time a

00:02:17.759 --> 00:02:23.600
lamb had broken its leg and they got the vet in to set it and he talked me

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.880
through what he was doing. That was really useful. Yes, my farm had sheep too.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.800
The farm was in a valley and they had a lowland breed called Suffolk's, although

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.759
the farmer said they'd had other breeds in the past. So were they bred for their

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:46.279
meat? Mostly, yes. They're quite big and solid. My farm was up in the hills and

00:02:46.279 --> 00:02:51.720
they had a different breed of sheep. They were Cheviots. Oh, I heard they're wolves

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.520
really sought-after. Yes, it's very hard-wearing and they use it for carpets.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.240
Right. I was interested in the amount of supplements they add to animals' feed

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:06.639
nowadays. Like, even the chickens got extra vitamins and electrolytes in their

00:03:06.639 --> 00:03:13.440
feed. Yes, I found that too and they're not cheap. But my farmer said some are

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.919
overpriced for what they are and he didn't give them as a matter of routine

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:22.720
just at times when the chickens seemed to particularly require them. Yes, mine

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.600
said the same. He said certain breeds of chickens might need more supplements

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.919
than the others but the cheap and expensive ones are all basically the

00:03:31.919 --> 00:03:40.559
same. So did your farm have any other livestock, Diana? Yes, dairy cows. I made a

00:03:40.559 --> 00:03:46.279
really embarrassing mistake when I was working in the milkshed. Some cows had

00:03:46.279 --> 00:03:51.279
been treated with antibiotics so their milk wasn't suitable for human

00:03:51.279 --> 00:03:56.800
consumption and it had to be put in a separate container. But I got mixed up

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:02.360
and I poured some milk from the wrong cow in with the milk for humans so the

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:08.119
whole lot had to be thrown away. The farmer wasn't too happy with me. I asked

00:04:08.119 --> 00:04:13.240
my farmer how much he depended on the vet to deal with health problems. I'd read

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:17.920
reports that the livestock's health is being affected as farmers are under

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.559
pressure to increase production. Well, he didn't agree with that but he said that

00:04:23.559 --> 00:04:28.320
actually some of the stuff the vets do, like minor operations, he'd be quite

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.720
capable of doing himself. Yeah, my farmer said the same but he reckons vet skills

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.920
are still needed.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.760
Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.959
at questions 27 to 30.

00:04:47.959 --> 00:05:11.920
Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30.

00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:18.880
Now we've got to give a bit of feedback about last term's modules, just short

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:26.079
comments apparently. Shall we do that now? OK, so medical terminology. Well, my heart

00:05:26.079 --> 00:05:32.600
sank when I saw that, especially right at the beginning of the course and I did

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:37.920
struggle with it. I thought it would be hard but actually I found it all quite

00:05:37.959 --> 00:05:44.119
straightforward. What did you think about diet and nutrition? OK, I suppose. Do you

00:05:44.119 --> 00:05:47.959
remember what they told us about pet food and the fact that there's such

00:05:47.959 --> 00:05:53.000
limited checking into whether or not it's contaminated? I mean, in comparison

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.200
with the checks on food for humans, I thought that was terrible. Hmm, I think

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.440
the module that really impressed me was the animal disease one, when we looked at

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.559
domesticated animals in different parts of the world, like camels and water

00:06:08.559 --> 00:06:14.480
buffalo and alpaca. The economies of so many countries depend on these but

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:19.679
scientists don't know much about the diseases that affect them. Yes, I thought

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:22.959
they'd know a lot about ways of controlling and eradicating those

00:06:22.959 --> 00:06:28.359
diseases but that's not the case at all. I loved the wildlife medication unit,

00:06:28.359 --> 00:06:33.399
things like helping birds that have been caught in oil spills. That's

00:06:33.399 --> 00:06:37.519
something I hadn't thought about before. Yeah, I thought I might write my

00:06:37.519 --> 00:06:42.760
dissertation on something connected with that. Right, so actually I was thinking

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.880
something along the same lines.

00:06:46.959 --> 00:06:52.839
That is the end of part three. You now have half a minute to check your answers

00:06:52.839 --> 00:06:55.679
to part three.

